# C3: teaching explanations for B1–B2 grammar + vocabulary. q -> (rule + example, [note per wrong option, in item order])
C3B = {
"b1/grammar": {
"If I ___ more time, I'd learn to play the harp.": ("Second conditional (unreal now): if + past simple, would + verb. Example: If I had wings, I'd fly.", ["Have is for real possibilities (first conditional), but this is a wish-like 'if'.", "Would never goes in the if-part.", "Will have is a future form; it doesn't fit an unreal 'if'."]),
"The forest path ___ by the fairies every spring.": ("Passive present simple: is/are + past participle. The path doesn't repair itself. Example: The room is cleaned every day.", ["Repairs is active: the path would be doing the repairing.", "Is repairing is active and about now, not every spring.", "Has repair isn't a correct form."]),
"She asked me where I ___.": ("In reported questions, the tense moves back and there's no question word order. Example: He asked what I wanted.", ["Live doesn't move back after 'asked'.", "No 'do' in a reported question.", "Am living is present and doesn't move back."]),
"I'm used to ___ up early.": ("Be used to + -ing = be familiar with. Example: She's used to walking to school.", ["After 'used to' meaning 'familiar', we need -ing, not the plain verb.", "Got is past; it can't follow 'to' here.", "Be getting isn't used after 'used to'."]),
"By the time we arrived, the owl ___.": ("Past perfect (had + past participle) shows the earlier of two past actions. Example: When I got there, the film had started.", ["Has flown is present perfect; the story is in the past.", "Flies is present.", "Was fly isn't a correct form."]),
"You ___ have told me! I would have helped.": ("Should have + past participle = a past action that was the right thing but didn't happen. Example: I should have brought an umbrella.", ["Must have is a guess about the past, not advice.", "Can have isn't used like this.", "Will have is future."]),
"The forest, ___ is very old, is full of secrets.": ("Commas around the clause = extra information; use which for things (not that). Example: My bike, which is red, is new.", ["That can't be used after a comma.", "Who is for people.", "What doesn't join clauses like this."]),
"I've been waiting here ___ two hours.": ("For + a length of time. Example: for ten minutes.", ["Since needs a start point: since 3 o'clock.", "During needs a noun: during the film.", "While needs a clause: while I was waiting."]),
"He suggested ___ the river path.": ("Suggest + -ing. Example: She suggested going home.", ["Suggest isn't followed by 'to + verb'.", "The plain verb can't follow suggest.", "Took is past and doesn't fit."]),
"Unless you hurry, you ___ the last boat.": ("Unless = if not; use it like a first conditional. Example: Unless it rains, we'll play.", ["The result part needs will.", "Won't miss gives the opposite meaning.", "Would is for unreal situations."]),
"She ___ be at home — her lights are on.": ("Must = I'm almost sure (deduction). Example: You must be tired after the trip.", ["Can't = I'm sure it's NOT true, but the lights suggest she IS home.", "Mustn't means not allowed.", "Needn't means not necessary."]),
"I wish I ___ speak to animals.": ("Wish + could for abilities we don't have. Example: I wish I could fly.", ["Can is present real, not a wish.", "Will isn't used for an ability wish.", "Would can is never correct."]),
"It's the most beautiful place I ___ visited.": ("Superlative + present perfect with ever. Example: It's the best book I have ever read.", ["Ever goes after have, not before it.", "Had never is past perfect and the meaning is wrong.", "Did ever isn't used with a superlative like this."]),
"We'd better ___ before it gets dark.": ("Had better + plain verb (no to). Example: You'd better hurry.", ["No 'to' after had better.", "No -ing after had better.", "Went is past."]),
"Neither Tom ___ Sara knew the way.": ("Neither ... nor. Example: Neither cats nor dogs can fly.", ["Or goes with either: either Tom or Sara.", "And goes with both.", "But doesn't pair with neither."]),
"The map was ___ old that we couldn't read it.": ("So + adjective + that = result. Example: It was so cold that we stayed in.", ["Such needs a noun: such an old map.", "Too goes with 'to': too old to read.", "Very can't be followed by 'that' to show a result."]),
"If it ___ tomorrow, we'll have the picnic indoors.": ("First conditional: if + present simple. Example: If it snows, we'll build a snowman.", ["No will in the if-part.", "Rained is for unreal second conditionals.", "Would isn't used in the if-part."]),
"The letter ___ before the queen arrived.": ("Past perfect passive: had been + past participle. Example: The cake had been eaten.", ["Has been is present perfect, not a past story.", "Was delivering is active: the letter can't deliver.", "Had delivered is active: the letter delivered something?"]),
"He told me he ___ the dragon the day before.": ("Reported speech moves back: saw → had seen. Example: She said she had lost her key.", ["Has seen doesn't move back after 'told'.", "Sees is present.", "Was see is not a correct form."]),
"I'm thinking ___ joining the archery club.": ("Think of/about + -ing = consider. Example: I'm thinking of learning piano.", ["Think to isn't used like this.", "Think for isn't used like this.", "Think at isn't used."]),
"You ___ wear a uniform, but you can if you want.": ("Don't have to = not necessary. Example: You don't have to come.", ["Mustn't = not allowed, but you can if you want.", "Haven't to isn't correct English.", "Shouldn't is followed by the plain verb, not 'to'."]),
"This is the village ___ my grandmother was born.": ("Where for places in relative clauses. Example: the house where I live.", ["Which would need 'in': which my grandmother was born in.", "Who is for people.", "What doesn't join clauses."]),
"She's been learning French ___ she was six.": ("Since + a starting point. Example: since Monday, since I was small.", ["For needs a length of time: for six years.", "From needs 'to' or 'until'.", "During needs a noun."]),
"I'd rather ___ at home tonight.": ("Would rather + plain verb. Example: I'd rather walk.", ["No 'to' after would rather.", "No -ing after would rather.", "Stayed is used when another person follows: I'd rather you stayed."]),
"The castle was ___ an amazing sight that we stopped to stare.": ("Such + (a/an) + adjective + noun + that. Example: It was such a good film that I saw it twice.", ["So goes before an adjective alone: so amazing.", "Too doesn't go with 'that'.", "Very can't start a 'that' result."]),
"He can't afford ___ a new horse.": ("Afford + to + verb. Example: We can't afford to waste time.", ["Afford isn't followed by -ing.", "We need 'to' before the verb.", "Bought is past."]),
"While I ___ through the forest, I heard a strange noise.": ("While + past continuous for the longer background action. Example: While I was cooking, the doorbell rang.", ["Walked is a short finished action; 'while' wants a longer one.", "Have walked doesn't fit a past story.", "Am walking is now."]),
"Do you know what time ___?": ("In indirect questions, use normal word order (subject + verb). Example: Can you tell me where the station is?", ["No 'does' inside an indirect question.", "Verb before subject is question order, wrong here.", "The 'does' isn't needed."]),
"It ___ be Tom at the door; he's on holiday in Spain.": ("Can't = I'm sure it's impossible. Example: That can't be true!", ["Mustn't = not allowed, not a deduction.", "Needn't = not necessary.", "Shouldn't is advice."]),
"I'll lend you my map ___ you promise to bring it back.": ("As long as = only if. Example: You can play as long as you finish your homework.", ["Unless = if not, the opposite meaning.", "Even though shows contrast.", "In case = to be ready for something that might happen."]),
},
"b1/vocab": {
"We had to ___ the picnic because of the storm.": ("Call off = cancel. Example: The match was called off.", ["Call up = phone.", "Put up = build or let someone stay.", "Take off = remove clothes, or a plane leaving."]),
"The fox was ___ to find its way out of the maze.": ("Unable to + verb = can't. Example: I was unable to sleep.", ["Impossible describes the task, not the fox.", "Incapable needs 'of': incapable of finding.", "Unlikely means probably not."]),
"I can't ___ up with this noise any longer!": ("Put up with = accept something annoying. Example: I can't put up with the cold.", ["Get up = leave bed.", "Keep up with = go at the same speed.", "Make up = invent or become friends again."]),
"Can you ___ after my plants while I'm away?": ("Look after = take care of. Example: She looks after her little brother.", ["Take care needs 'of': take care of.", "Care for means love or nurse, not 'care after'.", "See after isn't English."]),
"The journey took longer than we ___.": ("Expect = think something will happen. Example: It was easier than I expected.", ["Wait needs 'for'.", "Remember is about the past.", "Wonder means ask yourself."]),
"She ___ a promise to return before sunset.": ("We make a promise. Example: He made a promise to help.", ["Do a promise isn't English.", "Take a promise isn't English.", "Had a promise isn't natural."]),
"The castle guard was very ___; he never let anyone break the rules.": ("Strict = makes people follow rules. Example: Our teacher is strict about homework.", ["Generous means kind with money or gifts.", "Cheerful means happy.", "Relaxed is the opposite of strict."]),
"We need to ___ a decision soon.": ("We make a decision. Example: It's hard to make decisions.", ["Do a decision isn't English.", "Say a decision isn't English.", "Get a decision isn't natural."]),
"I'm really ___ forward to the festival.": ("Look forward to = be happy about something coming. Example: I look forward to seeing you.", ["Wait forward isn't English.", "See forward isn't English.", "Hope forward isn't English."]),
"The old bridge is ___ — please don't cross it.": ("After is we need an adjective: dangerous. Example: That road is dangerous.", ["Dangerously is an adverb: drive dangerously.", "Danger is a noun.", "Endangered is for animals at risk of dying out."]),
"Which word means the same as 'enormous'?": ("Enormous = huge = very big. Example: a huge whale.", ["Tiny is the opposite.", "Narrow means not wide.", "Ordinary means normal."]),
"The wizard's ___ was that it would rain for a week.": ("A prediction says what will happen. Example: The weather prediction was right.", ["A prescription is from a doctor.", "Production means making things.", "Protection keeps you safe."]),
"Could you ___ me how to get to the lake?": ("Tell + person + how. Example: Tell me your name.", ["Say isn't followed directly by 'me'.", "Speak isn't followed by 'me how'.", "Explain needs 'to': explain to me how."]),
"He ___ the bus, so he walked to the village.": ("Miss = be too late for transport. Example: I missed the train.", ["Lose is for things you can't find.", "Fail is for tests.", "Lack means not have enough."]),
"That song ___ me of my summer holidays.": ("Remind someone of = make them think of. Example: You remind me of my sister.", ["Remember doesn't take 'me of'.", "Recall doesn't take 'me of'.", "Memorise means learn by heart."]),
"We've run ___ of milk. Can you buy some?": ("Run out of = have none left. Example: We ran out of time.", ["Run off = run away.", "Run down = lose power or criticise.", "Run away = escape."]),
"Could you ___ down the music? I'm trying to study.": ("Turn down = make quieter. Example: Turn down the TV.", ["Put down = place on a surface.", "Get down = come down or make sad.", "Take down = write or remove."]),
"The festival was a great ___; thousands of people came.": ("A success = something that goes well. Example: The party was a success.", ["A winner is a person.", "A victory is for a battle or game.", "A profit is money earned."]),
"I ___ my dad; we both love painting.": ("Take after = be like an older family member. Example: She takes after her mum.", ["Look after = care for.", "Take up = start a hobby.", "Look up to = admire."]),
"The hotel room was ___: clean, quiet and with a lake view.": ("Comfortable = nice to be in. Example: a comfortable bed.", ["Comforting means making you feel less sad.", "Comfort is a noun or verb.", "Comfortably is an adverb."]),
"Let's ___ a break — we've been walking for hours.": ("Take a break = rest. Example: Let's take a break.", ["Do a break isn't English.", "Get a break means a lucky chance.", "Put a break isn't English."]),
"She's very ___; she always tells the truth.": ("Honest = tells the truth. Example: an honest answer.", ["Polite = good manners.", "Patient = can wait calmly.", "Modest = doesn't boast."]),
"The price ___ breakfast and dinner.": ("Include = have as part of the whole. Example: The ticket includes a drink.", ["Contain is for what's inside a container.", "Consist needs 'of'.", "Involve means take part or need."]),
"I ___ to get the job, but I'm going to keep trying.": ("Fail to = not succeed. Example: He failed to reach the top.", ["Miss isn't followed by 'to get'.", "Lose isn't followed by 'to'.", "Refuse means say no on purpose."]),
"Please ___ in mind that the gates close at six.": ("Bear in mind = remember. Example: Bear in mind it might rain.", ["Take in mind isn't the phrase.", "Hold in mind isn't the phrase.", "Have in mind means plan or think of."]),
"The road was ___ because of the flood.": ("Blocked = nobody can pass. Example: A tree blocked the road.", ["Locked needs a key.", "Shut up means be quiet (rude).", "Stuck means can't move."]),
"He ___ his ankle while playing football.": ("Twist your ankle = hurt it by turning it. Example: I twisted my ankle on the stairs.", ["Break down is for machines.", "Damage is for things, not body parts.", "Hurt himself can't be followed by 'his ankle'."]),
"Which word means 'very tired'?": ("Exhausted = very, very tired. Example: I was exhausted after the race.", ["Annoyed = a bit angry.", "Confused = not understanding.", "Amazed = very surprised."]),
"We set ___ early to avoid the traffic.": ("Set off = start a journey. Example: We set off at dawn.", ["Set up = start a business or prepare.", "Set down = put down.", "Set in = begin and continue (bad weather)."]),
"She ___ an effort to talk to everyone at the party.": ("Make an effort = try hard. Example: Make an effort to listen.", ["Do an effort isn't English.", "Take an effort isn't English.", "Give an effort isn't natural."]),
},
"b2/grammar": {
"Had I known about the storm, I ___ the trip.": ("Inverted third conditional: Had I + past participle, would have + past participle. Example: Had I seen you, I'd have waved.", ["Would cancel is about now; the storm was in the past.", "Had cancelled can't be the result part.", "Will have isn't used in conditionals about the past."]),
"The bridge is thought ___ over 500 years ago.": ("Passive reporting about the past: is thought to have been + past participle. Example: He is said to have been a king.", ["To be built is about now or the future, not 500 years ago.", "Being built doesn't follow 'is thought'.", "'That it was built' needs 'It is thought that...'."]),
"I'd rather you ___ tell anyone.": ("Would rather + another person + past simple. Example: I'd rather you stayed.", ["Don't is present; this structure needs the past.", "Wouldn't isn't used here.", "Not alone needs 'didn't'."]),
"She denied ___ the crystal.": ("Deny + -ing. Example: He denied breaking the vase.", ["Deny isn't followed by 'to'.", "The plain verb can't follow deny.", "'That take' is ungrammatical."]),
"By next summer, they ___ the new castle.": ("Future perfect: will have + past participle = done before a future time. Example: By 6 pm I'll have finished.", ["Will finish doesn't show 'done before'.", "Finish needs a future form.", "Are finishing doesn't fit 'by next summer'."]),
"It's high time we ___ home.": ("It's high time + past simple. Example: It's high time you went to bed.", ["Present go isn't used after 'high time'.", "Are going isn't used here.", "Will go isn't used here."]),
"The swans, ___ feathers shimmered, glided past.": ("Whose shows possession. Example: the girl whose bike was stolen.", ["Which can't show possession here.", "Who's = who is.", "That can't follow a comma or show possession."]),
"He must ___ the wrong path; he's still not here.": ("Must have + past participle = a strong guess about the past. Example: She must have forgotten.", ["Must take is about the present or future.", "Had taken can't follow must.", "Taking can't follow must."]),
"I'm not used to ___ so much attention.": ("Be used to + -ing. Example: I'm used to living alone.", ["Plain verb can't follow 'used to' meaning familiar.", "Received can't follow 'to' here.", "Have received doesn't fit."]),
"She'd have won the race if she ___ fallen.": ("Third conditional: if + had (not) + past participle. Example: If he hadn't slept in, he'd have caught the bus.", ["Didn't is second conditional.", "Would never goes in the if-part.", "Hasn't is present perfect."]),
"___ by the long journey, the knight fell asleep.": ("A past participle clause can give the reason: Exhausted = because he was exhausted. Example: Tired by the walk, we sat down.", ["Exhausting describes what tires you, not the knight.", "Having exhaust is not a correct form.", "Exhaust is a plain verb or a noun."]),
"You ___ have brought a gift, but it's very kind of you.": ("Needn't have + past participle = you did it, but it wasn't necessary. Example: You needn't have waited.", ["Mustn't have isn't used for the past.", "Couldn't have = impossible.", "Shouldn't have = it was wrong, which is unkind here."]),
"She's one of the few people ___ can solve the riddle.": ("Who for people as the subject. Example: the man who lives next door.", ["Which is for things.", "Whom is an object; here it's the subject.", "Whose shows possession."]),
"I regret ___ you that the boat has sunk.": ("Regret to tell/inform = sorry to give bad news now. Example: We regret to inform you...", ["Regret telling = sorry you told someone in the past.", "Plain verb can't follow regret.", "Told can't follow regret."]),
"The more you practise, ___ you become.": ("The + comparative, the + comparative. Example: The sooner, the better.", ["The second half needs 'the' too.", "Best is a superlative, not a comparative.", "More better is a double comparative."]),
"Not until the lake froze ___ how cold it was.": ("After 'Not until...' at the start, invert: did + subject + verb. Example: Not until midnight did she arrive.", ["Normal word order isn't allowed after 'Not until'.", "We did realise has the wrong order.", "Had realised we is jumbled."]),
"Whatever happens, we ___ to the plan.": ("Whatever + present, will + verb for the future. Example: Whatever you say, I'll go.", ["Would is for unreal situations.", "Stuck is past.", "Had stuck is past perfect."]),
"If she hadn't missed the ferry, she ___ here by now.": ("Mixed conditional: past cause, present result (would be). Example: If I'd eaten breakfast, I wouldn't be hungry now.", ["Would have been is a past result, but 'by now' is present.", "Will be is first conditional.", "Had been isn't a result form."]),
"The results ___ until next week.": ("Future passive: won't be + past participle. Example: The winner will be announced tomorrow.", ["Aren't announcing is active.", "Won't announce is active: results can't announce.", "Haven't announced is active."]),
"I wish you ___ interrupting me!": ("Wish + would = we want someone to change an annoying habit. Example: I wish he would stop shouting.", ["Stopped is for situations, not annoying behaviour.", "Had stopped is about the past.", "Will isn't used after wish."]),
"He's believed ___ abroad last year.": ("Is believed to have + past participle for the past. Example: She's thought to have left.", ["To move is present or future.", "Moving doesn't follow 'is believed'.", "To be moved is passive: someone moved him."]),
"___ the map more carefully, we wouldn't have got lost.": ("Inverted third conditional: Had we + past participle. Example: Had I known, I'd have helped.", ["If we read needs 'had': If we had read.", "Were we reading is second conditional style.", "Should we read is for the future."]),
"She insisted ___ for the meal.": ("Insist on + -ing. Example: He insisted on helping.", ["Insist isn't followed by 'to'.", "Insist needs 'on' first.", "Insist for isn't correct."]),
"By the time you get this letter, I ___ the mountains.": ("Future perfect for an action finished before a future moment. Example: By Friday I'll have read it.", ["Will cross doesn't show 'already done'.", "Am crossing is now.", "Have crossed is present perfect."]),
"They had the old tower ___ last spring.": ("Have something + past participle = someone else does it for you. Example: I had my hair cut.", ["Restore is the plain verb.", "Restoring is -ing.", "To restore doesn't follow 'had the tower'."]),
"Not only ___ late, but he also forgot the tickets.": ("After 'Not only' at the start, invert: was he. Example: Not only is she kind, but she's also funny.", ["He was is normal order; inversion is needed.", "He is is present and normal order.", "Did he be isn't English."]),
"You needn't ___ me a lift; I could have walked.": ("Needn't have + past participle = done, but not necessary. Example: You needn't have cooked.", ["Give is present.", "Needn't isn't followed by 'to'.", "Gave can't follow needn't."]),
"There's no point ___ about it now.": ("There's no point + -ing. Example: There's no point crying.", ["No 'to' after 'no point'.", "Plain verb doesn't fit.", "Worried is an adjective or past."]),
"The guide, ___ we'd met the day before, showed us a shortcut.": ("Whom = a person as the object (we met him). Example: the friend whom I visited.", ["Which is for things.", "Whose shows possession.", "What doesn't join clauses."]),
"Supposing you ___ the lottery, what would you do?": ("Supposing works like 'if' in the second conditional: past simple. Example: Supposing it snowed, would we go?", ["Win is first conditional, but 'would' needs the past.", "Will isn't used after supposing.", "Had won is for the past (third conditional)."]),
"It's no use ___ to him; he never listens.": ("It's no use + -ing. Example: It's no use crying.", ["No 'to' after 'no use'.", "Plain verb doesn't fit.", "Talked is past."]),
},
"b2/vocab": {
"The queen's speech had a profound ___ on everyone.": ("Have an impact on = have a strong effect on. Example: The film had an impact on me.", ["Affect is a verb; we need a noun.", "A result doesn't go with 'on everyone'.", "Consequence takes 'of', not 'have a ... on'."]),
"After months of practice, her efforts finally paid ___.": ("Pay off = bring success. Example: All that training paid off.", ["Pay out = give money.", "Pay back = return money.", "Pay up = pay money you owe."]),
"The evidence was ___; nobody could argue with it.": ("Overwhelming = very strong, too much to resist. Example: an overwhelming victory.", ["Overwhelmed describes a person's feeling.", "Overdone = cooked too long or exaggerated.", "Overcome is a verb: overcome a problem."]),
"We need to ___ the pros and cons before deciding.": ("Weigh up = think carefully about. Example: Weigh up your choices.", ["Look up = find in a book or online.", "Bring up = mention or raise a child.", "Turn up = arrive."]),
"He's ___ to win — he's the best by far.": ("Bound to = certain to. Example: It's bound to rain.", ["Liable to = likely to do something bad.", "Prone to = often affected by (illness, mistakes).", "Keen to = wants to, not certain."]),
"Swimming is ___ prohibited in the north lake.": ("Strictly prohibited = completely forbidden (a fixed phrase). Example: Smoking is strictly prohibited.", ["Strongly goes with recommend or believe.", "Hardly means almost not.", "Deeply goes with feelings."]),
"The new rules will come into ___ next month.": ("Come into force = start to apply (laws). Example: The law came into force in May.", ["Come into power is for governments.", "Put into practice is the phrase with practice.", "Come into play = start to matter."]),
"She ___ out of the competition due to injury.": ("Pull out = withdraw. Example: He pulled out of the race.", ["Break out = escape or start suddenly.", "Fall out = argue.", "Give out = stop working or hand out."]),
"I can't ___ the difference between the two crystals.": ("Tell the difference = see what's different. Example: I can't tell the twins apart.", ["Say the difference isn't the phrase.", "Speak the difference isn't English.", "Know the difference means understand it, not notice it."]),
"The manager ___ the blame for the mistake.": ("Take the blame = accept responsibility. Example: She took the blame.", ["Make the blame isn't English.", "Do the blame isn't English.", "Put the blame on = blame someone else."]),
"There has been a sharp ___ in the number of visitors.": ("A rise (noun) = an increase. Example: a rise in prices.", ["Raise is mostly a verb (raise your hand).", "Arise is a verb = appear.", "Rouse is a verb = wake up."]),
"He has a reputation ___ being honest.": ("A reputation for. Example: a reputation for kindness.", ["Of isn't used with reputation like this.", "About isn't used.", "With isn't used."]),
"Her explanation was ___; I understood instantly.": ("Crystal clear = very easy to understand. Example: The rules were crystal clear.", ["Sky high = very high (prices).", "Stone cold = completely cold.", "Brand new = completely new."]),
"We managed to ___ the problem by working together.": ("Overcome = defeat a difficulty. Example: She overcame her fear.", ["Overtake = pass a car.", "Overlook = not notice.", "Overdo = do too much."]),
"He's very ___; he always finishes what he starts.": ("Determined (adjective) = won't give up. Example: a determined climber.", ["Determining is a verb form.", "Determination is a noun.", "Determine is a verb."]),
"Which word is the opposite of 'reluctant'?": ("Reluctant = not wanting to; willing = happy to. Example: She was willing to help.", ["Hesitant is similar to reluctant.", "Unwilling means the same as reluctant.", "Doubtful means not sure."]),
"Let's try to ___ a compromise.": ("Reach a compromise / agreement. Example: They reached an agreement.", ["Arrive needs 'at': arrive at a compromise.", "Get a compromise isn't the usual phrase.", "Do a compromise isn't English."]),
"The new bridge will ___ traffic in the town centre.": ("Ease traffic = make it less heavy. Example: Medicine eased the pain.", ["Lighten is for loads or colours.", "Soften is for materials or attitudes.", "Loosen is for knots or belts."]),
"Despite the setbacks, the team ___ to finish on time.": ("Manage to + verb = succeed in something hard. Example: I managed to finish.", ["Succeed needs 'in + -ing'.", "Achieve takes a noun: achieve a goal.", "Accomplish takes a noun too."]),
"Her argument was so ___ that everyone agreed.": ("Compelling = very convincing. Example: compelling evidence.", ["Compulsive = can't stop doing it.", "Competing = trying to win against others.", "Compulsory = you must do it."]),
"We'll need to ___ costs if we want to stay in business.": ("Cut back on = reduce. Example: Cut back on sugar.", ["Cut out of isn't used with costs.", "Cut off from = separated.", "Cut up with isn't a phrase here."]),
"He's a ___ reader; he finishes a book every two days.": ("A keen reader = loves reading. Example: a keen gardener.", ["Sharp = clever or pointed.", "Strong isn't used with reader this way.", "Heavy reader isn't the phrase (heavy eater is)."]),
"Visits to the tower are ___ appointment only.": ("By appointment = only if you book. Example: Open by appointment.", ["On appointment isn't the phrase.", "At appointment isn't used.", "Under appointment isn't used."]),
"The decision was made ___ consultation with local residents.": ("In consultation with = after discussing with. Example: in consultation with teachers.", ["On consultation isn't the phrase.", "By consultation isn't usual.", "At consultation isn't used."]),
"She was ___ with the job of organising the festival.": ("Entrusted with = given an important job. Example: entrusted with the keys.", ["Entitled to = have a right to.", "Enrolled in = joined a course.", "Endorsed = publicly supported."]),
"His performance ___ all our expectations.": ("Exceed expectations = be better than expected. Example: The trip exceeded our hopes.", ["Excel = be very good at (no object like this).", "Exaggerate = make bigger than true.", "Expand = grow larger."]),
"It's ___ that she'll accept; she seemed very keen.": ("Highly likely = very probable (collocation). Example: It's highly likely to snow.", ["Strongly likely isn't a collocation.", "Deeply likely isn't a collocation.", "Greatly likely isn't a collocation."]),
"The company has ___ a new policy on remote working.": ("Adopt a policy = start using it. Example: The school adopted new rules.", ["Adapt = change to suit.", "Adjust = change a little.", "Admit = say something is true."]),
"Which word means 'to make something less serious or painful'?": ("Alleviate = make less painful. Example: Rest alleviates tiredness.", ["Aggravate = make worse.", "Accumulate = collect over time.", "Allocate = give out for a purpose."]),
"The two reports are ___ different; you can't compare them.": ("An adverb (fundamentally) describes the adjective 'different'. Example: completely different.", ["Fundamental is an adjective; we need an adverb.", "Fundamentalist is a person or belief.", "Fundament isn't the word needed."]),
"They ___ a heated argument about money.": ("Get into an argument = start one. Example: They got into trouble.", ["Get over = recover from.", "Get across = make understood.", "Get by = manage with little."]),
},
}
